{{also|précaution}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fr|précaution}}, {{uder|en|la|praecautio}}, from {{m|la|praecavere}}, {{m|la|praecautum||to guard against beforehand}}; {{m|la|prae||before}} + {{m|la|cavere||be on one's guard}}. See {{m|en|pre-}}, and {{m|en|caution}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pɹiːˈkɔːʃən/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/pɹiːˈkɔː.ʃən/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/pɹiːˈkɔ.ʃən/}}
* {{a|cot-caught|northern cities vowel shift}} {{IPA|en|/pɹiːˈkɑ.ʃən/|[pɹiːˈkɑ.ʃn̩]|/pɹɪˈkɑ.ʃən/|[pɹɪˈkɑ.ʃn̩]}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-precaution.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{hyph|en|pre|cau|tion}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Previous caution or care; caution previously employed to prevent [[misfortune]] or to secure good
#: {{ux|en|his life was saved by '''precaution'''}}
#* '''July 2, 1826''', {{w|John Henry Newman}}, ''The Philosophical Temper, First Enjoined by the Gospel''
#*: The ancient philosophers treasured up their supposed discoveries with miserable '''precaution'''.
# A measure taken beforehand to ward off evil or secure good or success; a precautionary act.
#: ''to take '''precautions''' against risks of accident''

